Investigation aim: Measuring the spread of stars' velcoities area within a cluster. 

The overall aim of this investigation is to figure out how internal velcoity dispersion depend on cluster mass 
and what clusters depart from the overall relationship?

Relationship for the velocity dispersion and stellar mass of clusters:

No. of clusters fitted: 157
Fitted equation: This has been measured from the codes variables of y = log(10)(sig_v2), x = log(10)(Mstar), y = mx + b
RMS residual: y = 0.915x + 1.122 this corresopnds to (sigma_v)^2 proportional to (M_asterix)^0.915

- There is an overall upward trend
- The more massive clusters generally have larger internal velocity dispersions.
- A noteciable scatter is present and mass does not discribe every cluster equally well.

For the residual graph:
- The positive residuals are indicative of a greater dispersion that what has been predicted. 
- The negative resiudals are indicative of a smaller dispersion.

Small results from the 20 outliers:

Cluster: NGC 5053. Residual: -0.919. Absoulte standarised residual: 2.75
Cluster: NGC 5466. Residual: -0.893. Absolute standarised residual: 2.67
Cluster: NGC 5024. Residual: -0.674. Abosulte standarised resiudal: 2.02

The pattern shows 3 large negative departures. The largest positive departure occur at NGC 6441 and NGC 7078 which are both around +0.49 dex.
Residual standard deviation := 0.334 dex.


Physical interpretations:

For similar sized bodies, greater masses would generally accomapy greater dispersion. 
- At a fixed mass it can result in more extended clutsers having smaller dispersions. Refence: https://academic.oup.com/mnrasl/article/359/1/L1/1034826?utm_source=chatgpt.com
The cluster sizes and concentrations can therefore be possible explanations for the investigated residuals 

Limitations:

- The measurement uncertainties are currently neither plotter nor used to weigh the fit
- The fit includes mss yet omits cluser size and structure.
- The internal velcoity disperstion itself cannot be enough to establish whether a cluster was accreted. 
